{L. W. Barden, a young Oxford student, is one of the most promising British masters. He very rarely has a downright bad result; and when he is playing in his best form the results are apt to be very good indeed, as when he won first prize at Paignton 1952 or tied for first with O’Kelly at Bognor Regis this year. He has an extraordinarily thorough knowledge of opening theory. This at times induces him to overrate the importance of a ’good position' in the opening, and as a result of his desire to play sound positional chess he used to neglect tactical complications, quite often drifting into sterile draws from hopeful openings. However, it was just against quiet scholarly Barden that I had to contest one of the most hair-raising endings I have ever been involved in in tournament play. Here is the position after the 36th move - the first session adjournment - of our game at the Cheltenham International tournament 1953. On resumption of play it turned out that Barden had sealed 37. g5! and play from the diagrammed position continued:-} 37. g5 fxg5 38. Rg3 Ke8 ({Here Barden - and his stablemate, Persitz - suggested after the game that I should have played} 38... Rf6 {, because they could not find anything tangible out of the sacrifice, 39 Bxg5! However, this is erroneous, for after} 39. Bxg5 $1 hxg5 40. Qh8+ Kf7 41. Rxg5 Qf8 42. Qh5+ Ke7 43. Rdg1 Rf7 44. Qg4 {it is difficult to see how Black could disentangle his pieces. The immediate threat is 45 f6+ gxf6 50 Rg8 Qh6 51 Qc8! and wins.} ({not immediately} 44. f6+ $2 gxf6 $1 45. Rg8 Rxb1 $1)) 39. h4 g4 40. Qxg4 ({The idea of the pawn sacrifice is to gain a move, either, as in the text, for Qf6 or, as after} 40. Rxg4 {, for} Nc2 {, with the continuation} 41. Bf2 Na3 {(possible because the rook has lost its pro­tection)} 42. Nc3 Rxd1+ 43. Nxd1 Kf8 44. Nc3 Rf6 {followed by ...Qd7.}) 40... Qf6 41. Bf2 ({Now the f7 rook joins its colleague on the a-file, from where it paralyses all White’s attacking chances. The alternative was} 41. Qh5 Be7 42. Rg6 Qxh4+ 43. Qxh4 Bxh4 44. Rxb6 Rfa7 45. Rb8+ Kd7 46. b6 R7a2 {and Black's mating threats may just about balance White’s passed pawns. Barden selects what he hopes will be a less critical line.}) 41... Rfa7 $1 42. Qh5+ Qf7 43. Rg6 Be7 44. Bxd4 ({White, threatened with having his whole king side forces cut off by Bf6, has to make haste to bring his knight into play. However, an immediate} 44. Nd2 {would allow} Rxd1+ 45. Qxd1 Nxf5 $1 46. exf5 Qxd5+ 47. Rg2 Ra2 {with a very strong bind on the White position and two ready-to-march passed pawns for the piece.}) 44... cxd4 45. Nd2 {Up to here, everything has been comparatively tame, but with the entrance of this knight the real fun starts.} Bxh4 46. Nc4 Kf8 47. Nxe5 Qc7 ({During the game Barden was afraid of} 47... Qe8 {, but whether this is superior to the text is doubtful. There could follow} 48. Rxa1 Rxa1+ 49. Kg2 Qxe5 ({the seemingly strong} 49... Bg5 $2 {would NOT win a pawn, for after} 50. Nf3 $1 Qxe4 $2 51. Re6 Qc2+ 52. Kg3 $1 {White wins} ({unless he in turn tries to be clever and plays} 52. Nd2 $4 Qxd2+ 53. Re2 g6 54. fxg6 Qd1)) 50. Qxh4 d3 {and now} 51. Rc6 $1 {leaves a pretty volcano for both sides to dance on. Why the last move is superior to Re6, let alone, the useless Q check, makes quite a fascina­ting bit of analysis, if some readers care to try their hand at it.}) 48. Nc6 R7a3 $1 ({Suddenly a ferocious attack against the white king has developed. Faulty would be} 48... R7a2 $2 49. d6 {, etc. The a2 square must be kept for the queen, whilst the black rook enters the king side via the 3rd rank.}) 49. d6 Qf7 50. Nxd4 {Now the threat of Ne6+ forces Black’s hand.} Rh3+ 51. Kg2 $1 ({At this stage both partners had seconds left on their clocks - but quite enough for White to see that} 51. Kg1 $2 {loses because of} Bf2+ 52. Kxf2 Qa2+ $3 {(anything else is refuted by an ultimate Ne6+)} 53. Qe2 Rh2+ 54. Rg2 Rxg2+ 55. Kxg2 Qxe2+ 56. Nxe2 Rxd1 57. e5 Rd5 {(JS note: other moves draw but this is a losing blunder)} 58. Nf4 Rxb5 {and wins. (Stockfish disagrees:} 59. Ne6+ $1 {wins for White.}) 51... Rg3+ 52. Kh1 $1 ({Again the only move, for if} 52. Rxg3 $2 Ra2+ $1 {followed by ...Qxh5. (Stockfish disagrees:} 53. Qe2 $1 Rxe2+ 54. Nxe2 Bxg3 55. Nxg3 {leads to a draw)}) 52... Rh3+ {Source: South African Chess Player, August 1954, ppn 7-8. Contributed by Andy Ansel, 1 July 2026.} ({Note that Black cannot play} 52... Rxg6 53. Ne6+ $1 Kg8 ({if} 53... Rxe6 54. Qxf7+ Kxf7 55. fxe6+ {and wins}) 54. Rxa1 $1 Rg1+ 55. Kxg1 Qxh5 56. Ra8+ Kh7 57. Nf8+ {with mate to follow. Thus both sides are forced to repeat moves.}) 1/2-1/2

