1. c4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. cxd5 cxd5 4. d4 Nf6 5. Bf4 Nc6 6. e3 e6 7. Nf3 Be7 8. Bd3 Nh5 9. Bg3 f5 10. Be5 Nf6 11. O-O O-O 12. Bxf6 Bxf6 13. Bb5 Qd6 14. Na4 Rf7 15. Rc1 Rc7 16. Nc5 b6 17. Nd3 Bb7 18. Qd2 Rac8 19. b4 Be7 20. a3 a6 21. Ba4 Bd8 22. Rc2 a5 23. Rfc1 axb4 24. axb4 Ra8 25. Bb3 Rcc8 26. Rc3 Be7 27. b5 Na5 28. Nfe5 Rxc3 29. Qxc3 Rc8 30. Qxc8+ Bxc8 31. Rxc8+ Bf8 32. Bc2 g5 33. h4 Kg7 34. hxg5 Nc4 35. Bd1 Nxe5 36. Nxe5 Be7 37. f4 Qb4 38. Kf2 Bd6 39. Be2 Bxe5 40. fxe5 f4 41. Rc7+ Kg8 42. Rc8+ Kf7 43. Rc7+ Ke8 44. exf4 Qxd4+ 45. Kg3 Qe3+ 46. Bf3 Qd3 47. Rc6 Qxb5 48. Rxe6+ Kf8 49. Rd6 Qb4 50. Bxd5 b5 51. Kg4 Qd4 52. Kf5 b4 53. g3 Qc5 54. Rd7 Ke8 55. Rd6 Kf8 56. Be4 Qc8+ 57. e6 b3 58. Kg4 b2 59. Rb6 Qc4 60. Rb8+ Ke7 61. Rb7+ Kd8 62. e7+ Ke8 63. Kf5 Qc8+ {Sources: CHESS, September 1953, Vol.18/216, p231 (full game score); (final position only, plus comments) BCM, September 1953, p248.} (63... Qc8+ {"White resigns, under the impression that} 64. Kf6 Qxb7 $4 65. Bxb7 b1=Q {would be fatal for him; but he could then have played} 66. Bc6# $1 {(CHESS). "The end of the game Mackay v Donner. Mackay had been trying for hours to win the intricate ending, allowing Donner's passed pawn to advance in the process. Now, after Donner had played Qc8+ with a terrific crash, the London player noticed nothing but the threatening promotion of the pawn - and RESIGNED. Actually, after 64 Kf6 Black has nothing better than perpetual check, for if 64...Qxb7?? 65 Bxb7 b1Q White administers a neat mate by 66 Bc6" (Wolfgang Heidenfeld, BCM, September 1953, p248)}) 0-1

