1. e4 e5 2. a3 Nf6 3. Nc3 Bc5 4. Nf3 d6 5. Bc4 O-O 6. d3 Nc6 7. Bg5 h6 8. Be3 Bb6 9. Qd2 Be6 10. O-O-O Nd4 11. Bxd4 exd4 12. Ne2 Bxc4 13. dxc4 Nxe4 14. Qe1 {Probably calculating that Black cannot now successfully defend the d4-pawn, e.g. 14...c5 15 Nexd4, securing the superior pawn position. White is so taken aback by Black's next move that he fails to realise that 15 Ned4 would have been a perfectly adequate defence. (CHESS)} d3 15. cxd3 ({Contrary to the contemporary note given in CHESS, White was lost from the moment that he played 12 Ne2. Now, if} 15. Ned4 {as suggested in CHESS, the engine shows that Black continues with} dxc2 16. Kxc2 Re8 {and it is all over for White. JS}) 15... Nxf2 16. d4 Nxd1 17. Qxd1 c6 {Black takes pains to get his pawns on to white squares, so as to provide his black-square bishop with maximum mobility; but for no fewer than ten moves he incomprehensibly shelves the development of his more important major pieces, by the end of which spell he has a lost game. (CHESS)} 18. g4 d5 19. Ng3 g6 20. Qd2 Kg7 $2 21. Rg1 Kh7 22. g5 f6 23. gxh6 Bc7 24. Nf5 gxf5 $2 {Losing by a forced continuation. If the knight is not taken, White has a much harder task.} 25. Rg7+ Kh8 26. Nh4 Qe8 27. Ng6+ Qxg6 28. Rxg6 Rf7 29. Qg2 Bf4+ 30. Kb1 Rh7 31. Rxf6 dxc4 32. Qh3 Bg5 33. Rg6 Bd2 34. Qh4 Rf8 35. Re6 ({Black's last move guarded against 35 Qf6+. A check to the knig on the long diagonal would finish him. There are other ways of engineering this check, however, and now} 35. d5 {would have won speedily, e.g.} f4 ({or} 35... Be3 36. Re6 f4 37. Qf6+ $1 {, etc}) 36. Qg5) 35... Rd7 36. Qh5 Rdf7 37. Qg6 Rh7 38. Qf6+ Kg8 ({Not} 38... Rxf6 39. Re8+ Rf8 40. Rxf8# {. White forces an exchange of rooks now to get rid of the potentially dangerous f-pawn.}) 39. Re8 (39. Qh4 Rxh6 40. Rxh6 Bxh6 41. Qxh6 f4 42. Qg6+ Kh8 43. Qh5+ Kg8 44. Qf3 {looks like an easy win - JS}) 39... Bxh6 40. Qg6+ Kh8 41. Rxf8+ Bxf8 42. Qxf5 Kg8 43. Qe6+ Kg7 44. Ka2 a6 45. Qxc4 Kh8 ({If} 45... Rxh2 $2 46. Qb3 $1 (46. d5 Rh6 47. Qg4+ {would be an excellent alternative})) 46. Qe6 Re7 47. Qf6+ Kg8 48. h4 Rh7 49. h5 {In the sequel, White makes clever use of divergent checks to protect loose pawns but, on the other hand, renders the win more laborious by playing pawns on to black squares (instead of white where they would be immune from the bishop).} Re7 50. b4 $2 Rg7 51. Qe6+ Kh8 52. Qe5 Kg8 53. Kb3 Rh7 54. Qe6+ Kh8 55. Qf6+ Kg8 56. Ka4 Re7 57. Ka5 Rg7 58. Kb6 Rh7 59. Qe6+ Kh8 60. Qc8 Kg8 61. a4 (61. Qxb7 {is worthy of serious consideration:} Rxb7+ 62. Kxb7 {; Black's remaining pawns fall and the bshop has more to do than he can cope with. Were White's pawns on white squares, this method would win very easily. (CHESS - Stockfish is less enamoured of 61 Qxb7)}) 61... Re7 62. a5 Rh7 63. Ka7 Re7 64. Kb8 Rg7 65. Qe6+ Kh8 66. Qe8 Kg8 67. Kc8 Re7 68. Qg6+ Rg7 69. Qe6+ Kh8 70. Qe8 Kg8 71. Kd8 Rh7 72. d5 cxd5 73. Qe6+ Kh8 74. Qe5+ Kg8 75. Qxd5+ Kh8 76. Qf5 Kg8 77. Qe6+ Kh8 78. Ke8 Bh6 79. Qg6 Bg7 80. Kf7 Bh6+ 81. Ke6 Bf8 82. Kf5 Re7 83. Kf6 Rh7 84. Qe4 Kg8 85. Qd5+ Kh8 86. h6 Be7+ (86... Rxh6+ 87. Kf7 {would lose Black a piece. (Stockfish in fact gives mate in 7)}) (86... Bxh6 87. Qd8+ {and mate next move.}) 87. Kg6 Bf8 88. Qc4 Re7 89. Kf6 Rh7 {Here the game was adjourned a second time. Courtney, who had repeatedly turned down offers of a draw, worked out a winning method in private analysis.} 90. Ke6 Re7+ 91. Kf6 Rh7 92. Qc8 Kg8 93. Ke5 Re7+ 94. Kd5 Rh7 95. Kc4 Re7 96. b5 axb5+ 97. Kxb5 Rh7 98. Kb6 Re7 99. Ka7 Rh7 100. Kb8 Re7 101. Qd8 Rh7 102. Qg5+ Kh8 103. Qf6+ Kg8 104. Qg6+ Kh8 105. Qe8 Kg8 106. Kc8 Re7 107. Qd8 Rh7 108. Qg5+ Kh8 109. Qf6+ Kg8 110. Qg6+ Kh8 111. Qe8 Kg8 112. Kd8 {The winning position that White has aimed at for the last twenty moves. The rook has to leave the second rank, and once the white king gets to e8, the b-pawn is lost and all is over.} Rxh6 (112... Rf7 113. Qe6 Bxh6 114. Ke8 $18) 113. Qe5 Rh7 (113... Rc6 114. Qd5+ Kh8 115. Qf7 $18) 114. Ke8 Bh6 115. Qf6 Bg7 116. Qf7+ Kh8 117. Qxb7 Bd4 118. Qb4 Ba7 119. Qf8# {Sources: CHESS, September 1953, Vol.18/216, p232; (move 92-end) BCM, September 1953, p247} 1-0

