1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e5 3. dxe5 Ng4 4. e4 Nxe5 5. f4 Nec6 6. Be3 Bb4+ 7. Nd2 ({Trying to eat his cake and have it too. A positional player like Andric does not fancy the split pawns resulting from} 7. Nc3 {- but then why play e4 in the first place! As a result of the text, the white pieces will get into each other's way. Black continues as in the main line, his next move weakening the white squares around the white king.}) 7... Qh4+ 8. g3 Qe7 {In order to stop the avalanche of advancing pawns if the black bishop is forced to exchange itself on c5.} 9. Qf3 a5 10. Bd3 Na6 11. Ne2 d6 12. O-O h5 $1 {This move caused quite a controversy between my opponent and myself after the game. He regards it as very weak - I consider it the move which the position demands. The idea is to be ready to exchange the knight on e2 if the necessity should arise - in other words, as soon as White, by kicking the black bishop away from b4, threatens to play his knight via c3 to d5. In addition, in some variations White will have to watch the further advance of this pawn.} 13. a3 Bg4 14. Qf2 $2 ({Overlooking Black's reply.} 14. Qg2 $1 {and now the simple} Bc5 $1 ({the tempting continuation} 14... Bxd2 15. Bxd2 Nc5 16. Bc2 f5 ({nor would the transposition of moves} 16... Bxe2 17. Qxe2 Nd4 ({if first} 17... f5 {, White could simply reply} 18. Bc3 {and recover the pawn with a strong position}) 18. Qd1 Nxc2 19. Qxc2 Nxe4 20. f5 $3 {be desirable - a possibility that also exists after the text.}) 17. exf5 $1 Bxe2 18. Rae1 Nd4 {would be refuted by} 19. Bc3 $1 (19. Bd1 $2 Nd3 $1 {and Black retains the exchange})) 15. Bxc5 Nxc5 16. Bc2 Bxe2 17. Qxe2 Nd4 {seems to give Black fine prospects.}) 14... Nc5 $1 15. Bc2 Bxd2 16. Bxd2 Nxe4 {Much stronger than the "deforestation" variation mentioned in the last note. The white attack is a chimera.} 17. Bxe4 Qxe4 18. Nc3 Qxc4 19. Rfe1+ Kd7 20. Re4 Qb3 21. Be1 ({The trouble is that any protection of the b2 pawn cuts off the a1 rook, whether the bishop goes to c1, e1 or e3. White should have foreseen this dilemma and played} 21. Rae1 {, though the game was lost in any case.}) 21... Rae8 22. Qd2 Rxe4 23. Nxe4 Kc8 24. Rc1 Re8 25. Nc3 g5 $1 {Either opening the g-file or gaining e5 for the knight. Faced with this desperate choice, Andric took over half an hour for his next - and then came up with a move that allows a mate in four!} 26. Kg2 $2 gxf4 $2 ({I was so eager on opening the g-file that I didn't stop to think:} 26... Re2+ $1 27. Nxe2 (27. Qxe2 Bxe2 28. Nxe2 Qxb2 {loses further material}) 27... Qf3+ 28. Kg1 Bh3 {and mate next move, would have finished immediately.}) 27. gxf4 Qc4 {Now I realised that I had missed the mate. With the g3-square open for the knight there's no trace of it left. Disappointed, I am now heading straight for the ending, regardless of whether there are any stronger continuations.} 28. h3 Bf5 29. Bf2 Rg8+ 30. Kh2 Be4 31. Rg1 Rxg1 32. Bxg1 d5 33. Kg3 Qf1 34. Qf2 Qd3+ {Exchanging on e3 instead of f2 stops the king from entering the black game because of the threat of a fork on f5.} 35. Qe3 Qxe3+ 36. Bxe3 Ne7 37. Ne2 Nf5+ 38. Kf2 Nxe3 39. Kxe3 h4 40. Nc3 Bg2 {The sealed move. During the adjournment I made sure that the pawn ending is an easy win and thus don't bother about the h-pawn.} ({The version of the finish given in a South African Magazine was} 40... Kd7 41. Nxe4 dxe4 42. Kxe4 Ke6 43. b3 f5+ 44. Kd4 Kd6 45. Kc4 c6 46. Kd4 b6 47. Kc4 b5+ {and White resigned on the 55th move.}) 41. Kf2 Be4 42. Ke3 Kd7 43. Nxe4 dxe4 44. Kxe4 Ke6 45. b3 (45. f5+ Kf6 46. Kf4 a4 $1 {leaves White no chance to speak of.}) 45... f5+ 46. Kd4 Kd6 47. Kc4 c6 48. Kd4 b6 49. Kc4 b5+ {and White resigned on the 55th move. Source: CHESS, September 1953, Vol.18/216, p230 (with Heidenfeld's own annotations)} 0-1

