1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. f4 d5 4. fxe5 Nxe4 5. d3 {Persitz calls this the 'Oxford' variation of the Vienna Game. "The last edition of 'Modern Chess Openings' indicates this move as allowing Black to get the upper hand by means of a continuation which is 'analysis by Barden and Penrose'! UnfortuÂ­nately, books take time to print and, almost before these words were in print, Barden and his friends at Oxford University had found a flaw in their own analysis." (Wood, ILN)} Nxc3 (5... Bb4 6. dxe4 Qh4+ 7. Ke2 Bxc3 8. bxc3 Bg4+ 9. Nf3 dxe4 10. Qd4 Bh5 11. Ke3 Bxf3 12. Bb5+ c6 13. gxf3 cxb5 14. Ba3 Qh6+ 15. f4 Qh3+ 16. Kxe4 Nc6 17. Qd3 Qg2+ 18. Ke3 g5 19. Rhf1 Qxh2 20. Qf5 Rg8 21. fxg5 Nxe5 22. Rad1 Qg3+ 23. Ke4 Rxg5 24. Qe6+ fxe6 25. Rf8# {Persitz-Hooper, Ilford 1954}) 6. bxc3 d4 7. Nf3 Nc6 8. cxd4 Nxd4 $2 9. c3 Nxf3+ 10. Qxf3 Be7 11. d4 O-O 12. Bd3 c6 13. O-O Be6 14. Qe4 g6 15. Bh6 Re8 16. Qe3 Qd7 17. Rf4 c5 18. Raf1 cxd4 19. cxd4 Rac8 20. Qf2 Bd8 21. Kh1 Rc3 22. Qe2 a6 23. h4 Bd5 24. Kh2 Qc6 25. R1f2 f5 26. Bxf5 gxf5 27. Qg4+ Qg6 28. Rxf5 Be7 29. Qf4 Rcc8 30. h5 Qxf5 31. Qxf5 Bf8 32. Qg5+ Kh8 33. Rxf8+ {Sources: Manchester Guardian, 20 August 1953; Illustrated London News, 22 August 1953; CHESS, September 1953, Vol.18/216, p231} 1-0

